 Bovine Shuffle  ·  USACO 2024 December, Silver P2
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Note: The time limit for this problem is 4s, twice the default.

  Farmer John has N cows (1 ≤ N ≤ 2 · 10⁵) standing in a line, the i-th of which has height hᵢ (1
  ≤ hᵢ ≤ 10⁹). Every morning he performs the same shuffle: for each i, the cow in position i moves
  to position aᵢ, where a[1], a[2], …, a[N] is given.

  A position is always occupied if some cow stands there after every shuffle, no matter how many
  shuffles have happened. Count the always occupied positions, and output ⌊S / 2⌋ mod (10⁹+7)
  where S is the sum of their heights.

  ### INPUT FORMAT (input arrives from the terminal / stdin):

  The first line contains N. The second line contains a[1] … a[N] (1 ≤ aᵢ ≤ N). The third line
  contains h[1] … h[N].

  ### OUTPUT FORMAT (print output to the terminal / stdout):

  Two integers: the count, and ⌊S/2⌋ modulo 10⁹+7.

  ### SAMPLE INPUT:

    4
    3 2 1 3
    5 1 7 2

  ### SAMPLE OUTPUT:

    3 6

  Positions 1, 2 and 3 stay occupied; S = 5 + 1 + 7 = 13 and ⌊13/2⌋ = 6.

  SCORING:

  • Inputs 2-5: N ≤ 100
  • Inputs 6-10: N ≤ 10³
  • Inputs 11-14: no additional constraints.
